syslogd: with both -C and -O, log to the memory buffer and the file

syslogd -C logs only to the memory buffer (read with logread) and ignores -O. The system log
goes to both: the buffer for logread, and a file on the system partition that survives reboots.
syslogd -C alone still logs only to the buffer.

Upstream: not submitted

--- a/sysklogd/syslogd.c
+++ b/sysklogd/syslogd.c
@@ -893,7 +893,9 @@
 #if ENABLE_FEATURE_IPC_SYSLOG
 		if ((option_mask32 & OPT_circularlog) && G.shbuf) {
 			log_to_shmem(G.printbuf);
-			return;
+			/* With -O as well: also to that file */
+			if (!(option_mask32 & OPT_outfile))
+				return;
 		}
 #endif
 		log_locally(now, G.printbuf, &G.logFile);
